{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Amara, the pertinent information is: executive: planning long written tasks; sensory: no primary concern; medical: none affecting school; and emotional: anxiety after repeated literacy failure. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: slow, effortful decoding; spelling under time pressure; copying notes while listening; and reading volume unrelated to the learning goal. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Amara, the most useful EA actions are to separate content knowledge from print mechanics; provide accessible text at the same conceptual level; teach—not only accommodate—literacy skills; and protect dignity and confidentiality. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-Q-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.4625, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.475, "end": 1.75, "start_char": 22, "e